## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit values without hardware helpers.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation as hi/lo
- Leading-zero counting
- Branching vs branchless approaches
- Correct handling of zero input

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo helper assuming a 64-bit lz routine exists as lz64
static int lz64(uint64_t v){
  int n = 0;
  if ((v & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) == 0) { n += 32; v <<= 32; }
  if ((v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 16; v <<= 16; }
  if ((v & 0xFF00000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 8; v <<= 8; }
  if ((v & 0xF000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 4; v <<= 4; }
  if ((v & 0xC000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 2; v <<= 2; }
  if ((v & 0x8000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 1; }
  return n;
}
int countLeadingZeros128(uint128 x){
  if (x.hi != 0) return lz64(x.hi);
  if (x.lo != 0) return 64 + lz64(x.lo);
  return 128;
}
```

